From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: feed pll-ref from the DCXO, not from SYS_CLK24M

PLL_REF in ccu-sun60iw2.c is registered with parent `{ .fw_name = "hosc" }`,
which the device tree gives as 24 MHz. Physically PLL_REF's input is the
crystal oscillator (DCXO, 26 MHz on this board); PLL_REF is precisely the block
that turns 26 MHz into the 24.000000 MHz that the peripheral PLLs then use as
their reference. With the wrong parent the driver computes

    24 MHz x 96 / 104 = 22.153846 MHz

where the silicon makes 24.000000 MHz, and every rate below pll-peri0 and
pll-peri1 is modelled 7.7 % low: the SMHC card clocks (patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-smhc-pll-peri1-clocks`), and the
ahb/apb rates stmmac reads for its MDC divider (patch `arm64-dts-a733-add-gmac0-ethernet`). Measured on this
board, ahb sits on pll-peri0-600m and would be modelled 184.6 MHz instead
of 200 MHz; apb0 92.3 MHz instead of 100 MHz.

Give the CCU a second input clock named "dcxo", wire it in the board DT to a
26 MHz fixed-clock, and move PLL_REF -- and only PLL_REF -- onto it.

Found while writing patch `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo`, which walks the oscillator chain; recorded
there under "WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX" and deliberately not folded in, because
the fix is a driver + binding change, not a DT re-rate.

== WHY ONE INPUT CANNOT SERVE BOTH NETS

The A733 has two physically different high-frequency nets and the UM names both
in one table, per module (A733 UM V0.92, section 7.1.4):

    SYS_CLK24M   the SoC-internal net, output of a multiplexer between DCXO
                 and PLL_REF, fixed at 24 MHz
    DCXO         the off-chip crystal oscillator, 26 MHz

and UM 4.1.1 lists the 26 MHz reference clock among the CCU's input signals.

So the CCU takes 26 MHz in and has a 24 MHz net inside it. A single DT "hosc"
input cannot describe both. Re-rating the existing 24 MHz node to 26 MHz --
the obvious reading -- does not fix this: it would move the 7.7 % error off
PLL_REF and onto the four mux slots that really are SYS_CLK24M (AHB, APB0,
APB1, APB_UART src 000), plus SMHC0/2, SERDES, usb2-suspend, and would leave
osc12M (= hosc/2, patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-usb2-clocks`) claiming 13 MHz. Patch `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo` walks that argument
in full and renames the node to say what it is. This patch is the other half:
the second input.

== EVERY CONSUMER OF "hosc" IN THIS DRIVER, AND WHETHER IT MOVES

Enumerated against the whole main series (`clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu` + `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-smhc-pll-peri1-clocks` + `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-usb2-clocks` + `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock` + `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-gmac0-clocks`),
not just patch `clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`. Exactly one moves.

  pll-ref (`clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`)                    MOVES to "dcxo". Reference 26 MHz per UM
      Table 4-2's "Reference clock" column and Table 4-3's REF_PLL row.

  ahb / apb0 / apb1 (`clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`)          STAYS. Mux index 000 is SYS_CLK24M (UM
      4.1.6 register descriptions). Not theoretical: APB1 @0x02002518 reads
      0x00000000 on the 6 GiB board and a second 6 GiB board, i.e. it is running on index 0 right now.

  apb-uart (`clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`)                   STAYS, and is load-bearing. APB_UART
      @0x02002538 = 0x00000000 = src 000, and the console has been legible at
      115200 8N1 on every boot of this series. A divisor computed for 26 MHz
      on a 24 MHz input is +8.3 %, far outside an 8N1 frame's ~3 % budget, so
      the working console is itself a measurement that this net is 24 MHz.

  smhc0 / smhc1 / smhc2 (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-smhc-pll-peri1-clocks`)      STAY. Mux index 0 = sys24M.

  osc12M = hosc / 2 (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-usb2-clocks`)          STAYS. The OHCI 12 MHz clock is 24/2;
      26/2 = 13 MHz is not a USB rate.

  serdes-phy-cfg (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock`)             STAYS. Mux index 0 = sys24M (index 1 =
      pll-peri0-600m is what USB3 selects).

  usb2-suspend (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock`)               STAYS. Mux {osc32k, sys24M}; the DT
      assigns 24000000 to force bit24 = 1. Re-rating "hosc" would have made
      that assignment ask for a rate its own parent can no longer produce.

  usb2-u2-ref, usb2-mf (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock`)       STAY. Gate-only on the osc24M[] parent
      array; mux/M default to 24 MHz (read on the 8 GiB board: 0x1348/0x1354 =
      0x80000000).

  res-dcap-24m (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock`)               STAYS, despite a vendor-derived comment
      that says "parent dcxo/24M". UM 9.1.5.5 lists the DCXO's dedicated PHY
      reference outputs as UFS and COMBOPHY0/1 + HDMI; a resistance-calibration
      24M gate is not among them, and its own name says 24M.

Consequence for the diff: the `osc24M[]` parent array is NOT renamed or
removed -- `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock` and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-gmac0-clocks` use it by name for the three gates above. A new
one-entry `dcxo[]` array is added next to it and only pll_ref_clk points at it.

Between this patch and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock` the `osc24M[]` array has no user, so a W=1 build of
the series in that window warns "osc24M defined but not used". Verified, and
accepted rather than papered over: the default build adds
-Wno-unused-const-variable (scripts/Makefile.extrawarn:127, the W=0 branch),
which is what a plain compile check uses, and marking the array __maybe_unused
would state something that stops being true three patches later.

Not in this driver, listed because `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo` raised it and it is not fixed here:
the r_ccu node (patch `arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus`) passes the same 24 MHz node as its "hosc", but
AHBS_CLK_REG bits[26:24] index 000 on the R-CCU is "DCXO" (UM 4.2.5.1,
A733 user manual) -- the crystal, 26 MHz. Latent today: the 6 GiB board reads AHBS
0x03000000, APBS0 0x03000001, APBS1 0x04000000, so index 0 is selected on none
of the three. It belongs to the `arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus` patch and to the R-CCU's own node, not to
the main CCU's driver.

== WHERE EACH NUMBER COMES FROM

Spec (A733 UM V0.92):

  * Table 4-3 "PLL Typical Configuration", REF_PLL row:
    DCXO 26, N(INT) 96, VCO 2496 MHz, OUT 24 MHz. 26 x 96 = 2496 and
    2496 / 104 = 24.000000 exactly.
  * The same table, PERI0_PLL row: DCXO column 24, N(INT) 100, VCO 2400 MHz.
    The DCXO column is the reference-input column: it reads 26 for REF_PLL and
    24 for PERI0/PERI1/DDR/GPU/VIDEO/VE. That is the whole two-net story in one
    table -- PLL_REF eats 26 and everything else eats PLL_REF's 24.
  * UM 4.1.4.1: XO_CTRL0_REG bit[15:14] reports which DCXO frequency is
    fitted; REFPLL runs at a fixed 24 MHz; some PLLs can take either
    DCXO or REFPLL as their reference.
  * UM 7.1.4 and 4.1.1, described above.
  * A733 user manual: PLL_PERI0_CTRL_REG offset 0x00A0, reset
    0x40126310, PLL_N default 0x63; PLL_PERI0_BIAS_REG 0x00B0 reset 0x00150000;
    PLL_DDR_CTRL_REG 0x0020; PLL_DDR_BIAS_REG 0x0030 reset 0x00150000.
    PLL_N raw 0x63 = 99 with Table 4-3's N(INT) = 100 is what fixes the field's
    +1 offset, which is what the driver's
    _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, ...) already models.
  * A733 user manual: CCU 0x02002000..0x02005FFF (16K), RTC
    0x07090000..0x07090FFF.

Silicon (register readback, 2026-07-22, `sunxi_dump` on the 6 GiB board and, read-only (dumps only), on a second 6 GiB board;
every value below is byte-identical on both boards
unless stated):

    CCU + 0x0000  PLL_REF      = 0xF8675F00
    CCU + 0x0010  (PLL_REF BIAS) = 0x00150000
    CCU + 0x0020  PLL_DDR      = 0xF9006300
    CCU + 0x0030  PLL_DDR BIAS = 0x00150000
    CCU + 0x00A0  PLL_PERI0    = 0xFF126310
    CCU + 0x00B0  PLL_PERI0 BIAS = 0x00150000
    CCU + 0x00C0  PLL_PERI1    = 0xFF126310
    CCU + 0x0500  AHB          = 0x03000002
    CCU + 0x0510  APB0         = 0x03000005
    CCU + 0x0518  APB1         = 0x00000000
    CCU + 0x0538  APB_UART     = 0x00000000   (the 6 GiB board)
    RTC + 0x0160  XO_CTRL0     = 0x983FB0F7 (the 6 GiB board) / 0x183FB0F7 (a second 6 GiB board)

Decodes, and what each one is worth:

  * XO_CTRL0[15:14] = 0b10 = 26 MHz on both boards (the two words differ only
    in bit31 CLK_REQ_ENB). The register's documented reset value is
    0x983F_10F7, i.e. DCXO_ST = 00 -- so 0b10 is the strap pads talking, not a
    default. This is the crystal, read out of the silicon.
  * PLL_REF 0xF8675F00: N field [15:8] = 0x5F -> 95 + 1 = 96, M field [22:16]
    = 0x67 -> 103 + 1 = 104. 26 x 96 / 104 = 24.000000. Those are exactly
    Table 4-3's N(INT) 96 and 2496/24 = 104, and 24 MHz in would give
    22.153846 MHz, which matches no row of Table 4-3 and no rate this board
    runs.
  * PLL_PERI0 0xFF126310: low 24 bits are the documented reset value
    0x40126310 unchanged; only the enable/lock/gate bits [31:24] are set. N
    field 0x63 -> 100, so 24 x 100 = 2400 MHz = Table 4-3's PERI0_PLL VCO
    exactly. With PLL_REF at 22.15 that product is 2215.4 MHz, a number the UM
    never mentions. This is the arithmetic cross-check that PLL_REF really
    delivers 24.000: PERI0 has to see 24, and PERI0's own reference column in
    Table 4-3 says 24, not 26.
  * AHB 0x03000002: src [25:24] = 3 = pll-peri0-600m, M + 1 = 3 -> 200 MHz.
    APB0 0x03000005 -> 600 / 6 = 100 MHz. Those are the two rates that carry
    the error into stmmac; both are exact round numbers only if PLL_REF is 24.

Base identification, stated as inference because the UM does not document the
register at CCU + 0x0000 (the CCU
register list starts at 0x0020 = PLL_DDR_CTRL_REG, and "PLL_REF" appears in the
document only as a clock name). Three facts converge on "a PLL sits at 0x0000":
a live PLL-shaped word with PLL_EN/LDO/LOCK_EN/LOCK/gate all set; CCU + 0x0010
holding 0x00150000, the identical BIAS pattern the two *documented* BIAS
registers at 0x0030 and 0x00B0 hold; and the decode above reproducing Table
4-3's REF_PLL row exactly. The field layout at 0x0000 is not the peripheral-PLL
layout -- reading [22:20] as PLL_OUTPUT0_DIV would give /7 and 356 MHz -- so
the 7-bit [22:16] divider the driver already models is itself part of that
inference. What is NOT inferred: the driver's existing field model, unchanged
by this patch, produces 96 and 104, and 96/104 is only self-consistent on a
26 MHz input.

No vendor BSP source, no sysfs, no clk_summary and no debugfs was used as a
source for any value above.

== WHY A DT FIXED-CLOCK AND NOT <&rtc_ccu CLK_RTC_DCXO>

Patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` (developed on a separate CPU-DVFS branch, not on main, when this was written) adds CLK_RTC_DCXO to the RTC-CCU, decoded
read-only from XO_CTRL0[15:14] on the running silicon. That is the better
source in principle -- it follows the straps instead of trusting a board
literal -- and this patch still does not use it. Two independent reasons, both
checked rather than assumed:

 1. A three-node cycle. Patch `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo` states that the main CCU cannot reference
    CLK_RTC_DCXO; verified against the nodes on main, not taken on trust:
    rtc_ccu (patch `arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy`) has `clocks = <&r_ccu CLK_R_AHB>`, r_ccu (patch `arm64-dts-a733-aon-pinctrl-usb-host-vbus`)
    has `clocks = <&osc24M>, <&osc32k>, <&iosc>, <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_200M>,
    <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_300M>`. Pointing ccu at rtc_ccu closes
    ccu -> r_ccu -> rtc_ccu -> ccu. The claim holds and it decides the design.
    Linux would survive it -- fw_devlink detects the cycle and relaxes the
    links, printing "Fixed dependency cycle(s) with ..."
    (drivers/base/core.c:2140-2143 in 6.18.36) -- but "the kernel copes" is not
    a reason to hand a reviewer a cyclic clock graph.

 2. Ordering. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` was on a separate CPU-DVFS branch and not on main. Depending on it would put
    this foundation fix behind the whole DVFS series for a defect that was live
    on main at the time. A dependency would be acceptable if stated; not having one
    is better.

Consequence, stated rather than hidden: once the DVFS work merges, two clocks
describe the same physical net -- "dcxo26M" from this board DT and "dcxo" from
the RTC-CCU. They cannot be one clock while the cycle exists. They must not
share a *name*: __clk_register() in drivers/clk/clk.c looks the name up
(clk_core_lookup) and returns -EEXIST for a duplicate, which for the RTC-CCU
would fail sunxi_ccu_probe outright. Hence "dcxo26M" for the DT node's
clock-output-names, with "dcxo" used only as the CCU node's *local*
clock-names entry, where no global namespace is involved.

The board literal is honest about being a board literal: it lives in the board
DTS, next to osc24M/osc32k/iosc, and it is backed by the schematic (X1 =
26.000 MHz, 16 pF, 10 ppm, p.8) and by the straps that report the same thing
back out of XO_CTRL0. A board strapped for 19.2 or 24 MHz gets a different
number in its own DTS -- which is what a board DTS is for. The CCU driver
cannot read the strap itself: XO_CTRL0 is at 0x07090160, inside the RTC block,
not inside the CCU's own window.

Rejected alternative, for the record: registering pll-ref as a plain 24 MHz
fixed-rate clock, on the strength of UM 4.1.4.1 stating that REFPLL runs at a
fixed 24 MHz. It removes the input entirely, and with it the gate bit 27 that
CLK_IS_CRITICAL exists to hold, and it would keep reporting 24 MHz on a board
whose boot0 programmed PLL_REF differently -- trading a wrong number for a
number that can no longer be wrong.

== THE BINDING

There is no schema for `allwinner,sun60i-a733-ccu` anywhere: mainline 6.18.36
has none, and no other patch in this series adds
one. A new input clock that exists only in a driver's fw_name lookup is not a
binding, so this patch writes the schema for the compatible it changes,
modelled on the A523 one. It documents exactly the four inputs the node has --
hosc, losc, iosc, dcxo -- and their descriptions carry the 24-vs-26 distinction
so the next person cannot make the same substitution.

== VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED

Verified, outcome inspected rather than exit code:

  * Applies clean with `patch -p1` at its position in the series as it
    stood in the working tree at 15:00 on 2026-07-22, into a fresh 6.18.36 tree -- and every later
    patch still applies after it, including `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-smhc-pll-peri1-clocks`, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-usb2-clocks`, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock` and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-gmac0-clocks`, the four
    that share ccu-sun60iw2.c. The CPU-DVFS patches `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`, `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo`, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu`, `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` and
    `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` also still apply on top of it. Not covered: patch `watchdog-sunxi-a733-add-wdt0`, which had been in
    the patches directory earlier and was gone by the time the
    apply test ran (the working tree was switched underneath). It touches
    drivers/watchdog and cannot collide with any hunk here, but if it also
    carries a DTS hunk, that context was not re-tested.
  * `drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/` builds for arm64 (defconfig, CONFIG_SUN60I_A733_CCU=y,
    gcc aarch64-linux-gnu) with no new warnings at W=0. The built object was
    inspected, not just the exit code: `strings ccu-sun60iw2.o` carries the new
    "dcxo" fw_name string alongside "hosc" and "pll-ref".
  * The dtb was built with cpp + dtc, zero warnings, and decompiled and diffed
    against the dtb built without this patch. The only changes are the new
    `dcxo26M-clk` node (phandle 0x19, clock-frequency 0x18cba80 = 26000000),
    the ccu node's `clocks` gaining that phandle and `clock-names` gaining
    "dcxo", and one unrelated phandle renumbering (uart0_pb_pins 0x19 -> 0x1a,
    consistently updated at its single reference). osc24M keeps phandle 0x16
    and stays "hosc".
  * The binding YAML parses (PyYAML). `make dt_binding_check` was NOT run --
    dtschema is not installed on this host. That is the reviewer's gate.

Not verified when this was written (2026-07-22): no kernel had been built and it had not been booted. Nothing here
was observed on silicon *as an effect of this patch* -- the register reads
above are the state of the hardware, which this patch changes how we describe,
not what it does.

== PENDING -- one falsifiable observation on hardware

One boot of the series with this patch on the 6 GiB board: in a single session,
`devmem 0x02002000` must read 0xF8675F00 and `devmem 0x02002500` must read
0x03000002, and in that same session

    /sys/kernel/debug/clk/pll-ref/clk_rate  =  24000000
    /sys/kernel/debug/clk/ahb/clk_rate      = 200000000

Before this patch the same registers give 22153846 and 184615384. debugfs is
the thing under test here on purpose: the defect is in the model, so the test
is whether the model now agrees with the arithmetic the registers force. Any
other pair falsifies either the "dcxo" wiring or the field decode. A wrong
number cannot pass by accident -- 24000000 is only reachable from
0xF8675F00 with a 26 MHz parent.

